---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import RagDiagram from '../../components/diagrams/RagDiagram.astro';
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Da Nang RAG assistant"
  summary="A grounded local-guide assistant over provenance-backed OpenStreetMap places, with a retrieval benchmark that decides which search strategy ships — including the one that won the table and was rejected anyway."
  metaDescription="Da Nang RAG assistant by Quoc Van Nguyen: hybrid BM25 + vector retrieval with RRF, a validated Gemini self-query planner, and a fingerprinted evaluation suite with paired bootstrap intervals."
  stack={[
    'Python',
    'Elasticsearch',
    'sentence-transformers',
    'Gemini',
    'Streamlit',
    'Docker',
    'Pytest',
    'GitHub Actions',
    'Grafana',
  ]}
  nextSteps={[
    'Rerun the generation evaluation under the production-aligned protocol. The current percentages come from a run that predates the query planner and the fixed candidate depth, so they describe an earlier system and are labelled that way below.',
    'Add geo enrichment for canonical district labels. Fail-open district filtering cannot show its recall benefit while area coverage stays sparse, so that part of the planner is unmeasured rather than proven.',
    'Human spot-check a sample of judge verdicts. Every generation number on this page is one model grading another, and nothing yet establishes that the judge agrees with a person.',
  ]}
  draft={false}
>
  <Fragment slot="links">
    <a
      href="https://github.com/koshiroquoc/DaNangtourguide"
      class="u-link inline-flex min-h-11 items-center font-mono text-[13px] tracking-[0.04em] text-accent"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      repo ↗
    </a>
    <span class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center font-mono text-[13px] tracking-[0.04em] text-muted">
      docker compose up · 95 tests in CI
    </span>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="problem">
    <p>
      Ask a general model where to eat in a specific Da Nang district and it will answer
      confidently with places that may not exist, at addresses it composed. Ask a listing
      site and you get a ranked page you have to read yourself. A retrieval-grounded
      assistant sits between those: it answers in sentences, but every claim has to come
      from a record.
    </p>
    <p>
      That only works if two things are true, and neither is true by default. The records
      have to be real — the first version of this project indexed a 299-row generated CSV
      and presented it as verified local data, which is the same failure mode as the model
      inventing addresses, just moved upstream. And the retrieval has to actually be better
      than the simple thing.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted">
      "We added hybrid search" is not a result. Without a benchmark on this dataset, there
      is no way to know whether combining keyword and vector search beats plain BM25 here,
      whether the LLM query planner helps or quietly drops constraints, or whether a tuned
      weighting is a real gain or noise someone decided to believe.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="diagram"><RagDiagram /></Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="diagram-caption">
    Fig. 02 — An OpenStreetMap base with a fill-null-only Wikivoyage overlay is indexed with
    per-field provenance; queries pass through a validated planner into hybrid retrieval, and
    the benchmarks are pinned to the dataset fingerprint they were run against.
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="evaluation">
    <div class="max-w-[60ch]">
      <span class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.08em] uppercase text-muted">Measured</span>
      <p class="mt-4 text-ink">
        Retrieval quality on two tracks that fail differently — discovery queries that name
        no venue ("somewhere for bún bò in Hải Châu") and name-lookup queries that do.
        Then the planner: whether Gemini's extracted filters match the intended ones. Then
        generation: whether the answer is faithful to the retrieved sources and actually
        answers the question.
      </p>
      <span class="mt-8 block font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.08em] uppercase text-muted">
        Eval design
      </span>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
        120 discovery and 90 name-lookup queries, six retrieval variants, candidate depth
        fixed at 20, embeddings from
        <span class="font-mono text-[13px]">all-MiniLM-L6-v2</span>. Every run is bound to
        the SHA-256 fingerprint of the dataset it was executed against, so a result cannot
        silently belong to a different snapshot. Variant comparisons use paired bootstrap
        over 5,000 resamples and are reported as intervals, not as which number is bigger.
        The planner is scored against an oracle condition that executes the intended filters
        perfectly, which separates "the planner is wrong" from "filtering does not help".
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 grid max-w-[720px] grid-cols-1 gap-6 sm:grid-cols-3 sm:gap-4">
      <div>
        <div class="font-mono text-[30px] leading-none font-medium text-ink">0.957</div>
        <div class="mt-2 font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.06em] uppercase text-muted">
          MRR@5 · validated self-query
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="font-mono text-[30px] leading-none font-medium text-ink">97.5%</div>
        <div class="mt-2 font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.06em] uppercase text-muted">
          Planner exact-filter match
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="font-mono text-[30px] leading-none font-medium text-ink">210</div>
        <div class="mt-2 font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.06em] uppercase text-muted">
          Benchmarked retrieval queries
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div
      class="data-scroll mt-12 max-w-[860px] overflow-x-auto"
      tabindex="0"
      role="region"
      aria-label="Discovery retrieval results. Scroll horizontally to see all metrics."
      data-scroll-label="Table · scroll horizontally →"
    >
      <table class="w-full min-w-[620px] border-collapse text-left">
        <caption class="caption mb-4 text-left text-muted">
          Discovery track · 120 queries
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-grid">
            <th
              class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-[0.06em] uppercase text-muted"
            >
              Variant
            </th>
            <th
              class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-[0.06em] uppercase text-muted"
            >
              Hit@1
            </th>
            <th
              class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-[0.06em] uppercase text-muted"
            >
              MRR@5
            </th>
            <th
              class="py-3 font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-[0.06em] uppercase text-muted"
            >
              nDCG@3
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">
          <tr class="border-b border-grid">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">BM25</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-muted">0.292</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-muted">0.425</td>
            <td class="py-3 text-muted">0.294</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-grid">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Vector</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-muted">0.583</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-muted">0.670</td>
            <td class="py-3 text-muted">0.562</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-grid">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-accent">Hybrid RRF — shipped</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">0.608</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">0.693</td>
            <td class="py-3">0.547</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-grid">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Weighted RRF, semantic 0.7</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-muted">0.592</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-muted">0.697</td>
            <td class="py-3 text-muted">0.590</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 max-w-[60ch] border-l border-accent pl-6">
      <span class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.08em] uppercase text-muted">
        The decision that mattered
      </span>
      <p class="mt-4 text-ink">
        Weighted RRF at semantic 0.7 has the best MRR@5 in that table. It is not what ships.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
        Against BM25, hybrid RRF gains
        <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">+0.269</span> MRR@5 with a 95% interval
        of <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">[+0.200, +0.339]</span> — robust.
        Against plain RRF, the tuned variant gains
        <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">+0.004</span> with an interval of
        <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">[−0.026, +0.028]</span>, which crosses
        zero. So the improvement is indistinguishable from resampling noise, and shipping it
        would mean carrying a tuned parameter for nothing. The repository keeps the simpler
        untuned strategy and records why.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div
      class="data-scroll mt-12 max-w-[860px] overflow-x-auto"
      tabindex="0"
      role="region"
      aria-label="Self-query planner results. Scroll horizontally to see all metrics."
      data-scroll-label="Table · scroll horizontally →"
    >
      <table class="w-full min-w-[620px] border-collapse text-left">
        <caption class="caption mb-4 text-left text-muted">
          Self-query planner vs oracle filters · 120 queries
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-grid">
            <th
              class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-[0.06em] uppercase text-muted"
            >
              Condition
            </th>
            <th
              class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-[0.06em] uppercase text-muted"
            >
              Hit@1
            </th>
            <th
              class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-[0.06em] uppercase text-muted"
            >
              MRR@5
            </th>
            <th
              class="py-3 font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-[0.06em] uppercase text-muted"
            >
              Precision@3
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">
          <tr class="border-b border-grid">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Baseline hybrid</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-muted">0.608</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-muted">0.693</td>
            <td class="py-3 text-muted">0.500</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-grid">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-accent">Validated self-query</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">0.942</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">0.957</td>
            <td class="py-3">0.831</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-grid">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Oracle filters</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-muted">0.942</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-muted">0.954</td>
            <td class="py-3 text-muted">0.836</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 max-w-[60ch]">
      <p class="text-muted">
        The planner lands on top of the oracle —
        <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">0.957</span> against
        <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">0.954</span> — so on this benchmark
        almost none of the remaining error is the LLM misreading the query. Paired against
        baseline it gains <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">+0.264</span> MRR@5,
        interval <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">[+0.196, +0.334]</span>. It was
        enabled by default because a gate written beforehand said it could be: complete run,
        exact-match ≥ 90%, and a paired interval strictly above zero.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 max-w-[60ch]">
      <span class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.08em] uppercase text-muted">
        Generation — historical baseline, not a current claim
      </span>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
        A 150-query sample was answered and graded by a separate Gemini judge on
        faithfulness and answer relevance:
        <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">96.7%</span> and
        <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">98.0%</span> PASS,
        <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">94.7%</span> jointly, zero FAIL and zero
        runtime failures.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
        Those numbers describe an earlier build. The run predates the production query
        planner and the fixed candidate depth, so it is retained in the repository as a
        labelled historical baseline and flagged for rerun rather than quoted as the current
        system's score. They are also LLM-judge verdicts, not human ground truth. Both
        caveats are recorded at the top of the report itself.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 max-w-[60ch]">
      <span class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.08em] uppercase text-muted">
        What failed → what was fixed
      </span>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
        Every non-PASS case is kept in the report with the judge's reasoning. The pattern was
        not hallucinated venues — it was unsupported scope. Asked for burger places, the
        model answered "Here are some places you can go for burgers in Da Nang", where the
        retrieved records carried no city field at all. Correct venues, invented location
        claim. Faithfulness scoring counts source attribution, so that framing sentence is
        what dropped the verdict.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
        Upstream, the generated 299-row CSV was demoted to a clearly-labelled legacy
        baseline and replaced with a reproducible Overpass snapshot plus a Wikivoyage overlay
        that may only fill nulls, never overwrite. Each accepted field stores its own source
        revision, license, timestamp and match score, and missing facts such as prices stay
        null instead of being generated.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</ProjectLayout>
